          ":question/math-academy-id": "q-104686",
          ":question/problem": "Which of the numbers below belong to  ${Q}_{0}^{+}?$\n\nI. $0$\n\nII. $\\sqrt{20}$\n\nIII. $-\\sqrt{25}$\n\nIV. $\\frac{1}{2}$",
          ":question/worked-solution": "Remember that ${Q}_{0}^{+}$ is the set of all positive rational numbers and $0.$\n\n- $0$ belongs to ${Q}_{0}^{+}.\\,\u2713$\n\n- $\\sqrt{20}=2\\sqrt{5}$ is not a rational number, so it does *not* belong to ${Q}_{0}^{+}.\\,\\times$\n\n- $-\\sqrt{25}=-5$ is a negative number, so it does *not* belong to ${Q}_{0}^{+}.\\,\\times$\n\n- $\\frac{1}{2}$ is a rational number because it is the ratio of two integers, and moreover, it is a positive rational number. Therefore, $\\frac{1}{2}$ belongs to ${Q}_{0}^{+}.\\,\u2713$\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"I and IV only\"."
