        ":topic/knowledge-points": [
          {
            ":db/id": 17592186054814,
            ":knowledge-point/canonical-example": {
              ":db/id": 17592186054429,
              ":question/id": "e557c534-7392-5c31-9e08-43347736716e",
              ":question/math-academy-id": "e-13138",
              ":question/problem": "Consider the following sets:\n\n$$\n\\mathbb N, \\quad \\mathbb Z, \\quad \\mathbb Q, \\quad \\mathbb R, \\quad \\mathbb C\n$$\n\nTo which of these sets does the number $3.25$ belong?",
              ":question/worked-solution": "![](images/67/67ef97be4b21a74feaae15f3ff30d5077fc1316874df8de79155316a621e4487.png)\n\nRecall the following:\n\n- $\\mathbb{N}$ represents the set of **natural numbers**.\n\n- $\\mathbb{Z}$ represents the set of **integers**.\n\n- $\\mathbb{Q}$ represents the set of **rational numbers**.\n\n- $\\mathbb{R}$ represents the set of **real numbers**.\n\n- $\\mathbb C$ represents the set of **complex numbers**.\n\nWith that in mind, we note the following: \n\n- The number $3.25$ is not an integer, so it does *not* belong to $\\mathbb{Z}$ nor $\\mathbb{N},$  so we can write\n\n$$\n3.25\\notin\\mathbb N, \\qquad 3.25\\notin\\mathbb Z.\n$$\n\n- However, notice that $3.25$ can be written as the ratio of two integers as follows:\n\n$$\n3.25=\\dfrac{325}{100}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $3.25$ is a rational number, and we can write\n\n$$\n3.25 \\in \\mathbb{Q}.\n$$\n\n- Every rational is a real and complex number. Consequently, $3.25$ belongs to both $\\mathbb R$ and $\\mathbb{C},$ and we can write\n\n$$\n3.25 \\in \\mathbb{R}, \\qquad 3.25 \\in \\mathbb{C}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$\\mathbb Q, \\mathbb R,$ and $\\mathbb C$ only.\""
            },
            ":knowledge-point/id": "3de9e532-559c-5fc1-a6ad-5fcd1ed77824",
            ":knowledge-point/title": "Identifying Special Sets Containing a Given Element"
          },
          {
            ":db/id": 17592186054815,
            ":knowledge-point/canonical-example": {
              ":db/id": 17592186054430,
              ":question/id": "ce8a56c1-709e-50e8-ac10-d3f92bf15db0",
              ":question/math-academy-id": "e-13139",
              ":question/problem": "Which of the numbers below belong to $\\mathbb{Q}^-?$\n\n- $-\\sqrt{3}$\n\n- $- 0.2$\n\n- $0$\n\n- $-\\sqrt{4}$",
              ":question/worked-solution": "Remember that $\\mathbb{Q}^-$ is the set of all negative rational numbers.\n\n- $-\\sqrt{3}$ is negative but not a rational number, so it does *not* belong to $\\mathbb{Q}^-. \\quad \\color{red}\\times$\n\n- $-0.2$ is negative, and it is a rational number because it can be written as a ratio of two integers:\n\n$$\n-0.2 = -\\dfrac{1}{5}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $-0.2$ belongs to $\\mathbb{Q}^-. \\quad \\color{green}\\checkmark$\n\n- $0$ is a rational number but not negative, so it does *not* belong to $\\mathbb{Q}^-. \\quad \\color{red}\\times$\n\n- $-\\sqrt{4} =- 2$, and it is a rational number because it can be written as a ratio of two integers:\n\n$$\n-\\sqrt 4 = -2 = -\\dfrac{2}{1}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $-\\sqrt 4$ belongs to $\\mathbb{Q}^-. \\quad \\color{green}\\checkmark$\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"II and IV only.\""
            },
            ":knowledge-point/id": "abc6b3ee-0251-5c9c-9358-bd192c434dd1",
            ":knowledge-point/title": "Identifying Elements Belonging to Scripted Special Sets"
          },
          {
            ":db/id": 17592186054816,
            ":knowledge-point/canonical-example": {
              ":db/id": 17592186054431,
              ":question/id": "e8b7d098-3cda-548e-b1d3-e1f2a87b59b6",
              ":question/math-academy-id": "e-13140",
              ":question/problem": "To which of the following sets do all the negative integers belong? \n\n- $\\mathbb{Z}$\n\n- $\\mathbb{Z}_0^+$\n\n- $\\mathbb{Q}^-$\n\n- $\\mathbb{R}_0^-$",
              ":question/worked-solution": "The negative integers are $-1, -2, -3, \\ldots$\n\n- The set $\\mathbb{Z}$ contains all the integers, including the negative integers. $\\quad \\color{green}\\checkmark$\n\n- The set $\\mathbb{Z}_0^+$ contains the positive integers and $0.$ So, it does not contain the negative integers. $\\quad \\color{red}\\times$\n\n- The set $\\mathbb{Q}^-$ contains all the negative rational numbers. Since all integers are rational numbers, $\\mathbb{Q}^-$ contains all the negative integers as well. $\\quad \\color{green}\\checkmark$\n\n- The set $\\mathbb{R}_0^-$ contains all the negative real numbers and $0.$ Since all integers are real numbers, $\\mathbb{R}_0^-$ contains all the negative integers as well. $\\quad \\color{green}\\checkmark$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, III, and IV only.\""
            },
            ":knowledge-point/id": "1d132801-b9cc-5c0c-a8fa-0c8b840c05bd",
            ":knowledge-point/title": "Identifying Sets Containing a Given Element"
          }
